The weak acid curve:

- starts at the same pH and follows almost the same path while the strong base is in excess;
- has its equivalence point at the same volume (25 mL), with a shorter steep drop and an equivalence pH **above 7**;
- levels off after equivalence at a much higher pH (about 5), because the excess weak acid and its conjugate base form a buffer.

(The sketch uses a weak acid with $K_a=1.8\times10^{-5}$.)

Water adds across the double bond in two ways:

- **2-methylbutan-2-ol**, $\text{CH}_3\text{C(OH)(CH}_3)\text{CH}_2\text{CH}_3$ (major product, tertiary alcohol);
- **3-methylbutan-2-ol**, $\text{CH}_3\text{CH(CH}_3)\text{CH(OH)CH}_3$ (minor product).
